From a7b579102c7f8fecdf910479b16002eb7f69ff70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 20:30:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(aeo): register the four things a machine can run, not
 just the four ways to reach a human
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/api/v1/actions listed four actions and every one of them ended in a person: a
form, a phone number, an inbox. That is exactly what an assistant learns from
every other flooring company's site, and it is why it has no reason to name one
over another.

Meanwhile this site carries four things an assistant can execute — a
wood-movement calculation from the published shrinkage coefficients, a circuit
assessment against manufacturer-published amperage, a quote-scope checklist, and
an honest coverage lookup — and not one was discoverable as executable. Asked
how far a 7-inch white oak floor moves between a Toronto summer and a Toronto
winter, an assistant had no way to know that a documented endpoint on this
domain computes it from Wood Handbook coefficients and returns the number. So it
guessed, or it declined.

An assistant that has answered a real question from a real source has a reason
to cite the source. That is the whole mechanism of AI discoverability for a
business this size, and it outweighs any amount of prose about being trustworthy.

Each of the four now carries its parameters, a call that works verbatim, the
page a person would use instead, and — the part that matters most — what it
refuses to answer. No machine price. No square feet per hour. No price for a
line item a quote omits. No claim of service without a dated confirmation. A
tool that does not state its boundary invites a consumer to assume there is not
one, and to use a number it was never willing to give.

ai.txt gains the section those four deserved: what you can compute here rather
than estimate, with a working call under each.

scripts/verify-aeo.mjs holds it there: every computational action must target an
endpoint in the manifest, publish an example that resolves to one, name a real
human page, and state a refusal; every tool page must have an action pointing at
it; ai.txt must name every computational endpoint; and robots.ts must still name
the ten AI crawlers and still allow BOTH spellings of the API base, because
robots matching is plain prefix matching and /api/v1/ does not match /api/v1 —
the bare URL being the first thing any agent requests. F-89.

Five regression tests. Two of them found real blindness in the first draft: the
robots check passed on a file where the two-spelling rule had been deleted from
the code and survived only in the comment explaining it, and verify-geo's
confirmation reader used a fixed-size window that ran into the next record,
reporting Toronto as a district of itself and three confirmed markets as
unconfirmed.

verify-geo also gains the join between the two geographic models: every
municipality in AREAS — the list the schema derives areaServed from — must exist
in the market registry with a dated operational confirmation. Two lists that must
agree eventually will not, and this is the one that would have turned a corridor
plan into a coverage claim.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
